@@s009 All the operations on LazyList
@@s010 Every operation of week 4, with what it does in a line and the real result on <code>l = LazyList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code>. Next to it is who wrote it: the teacher in the file, or you in an exercise.
@@s011 Who wrote what
@@s012 Already in the file (teacher)
@@s013 In the <code>enum LazyList</code>: <code>Empty</code>, <code>Cons</code>, <code>headOption</code>, <code>tail</code>, <code>foldRight</code>, <code>foldLeft</code>, <code>find</code>.
@@s014 In the companion <code>object LazyList</code>: <code>empty</code>, <code>cons</code>, <code>apply</code> (it is what you use when writing <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3)</code>).
@@s015 Written by you
@@s016 <code>toList</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>drop</code>, <code>takeWhile</code>, <code>forAll</code>, <code>exists</code>, <code>takeWhile1</code>, <code>headOption1</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>append</code>, <code>flatMap</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>naturals</code>, <code>fibs</code>, <code>unfold</code>, <code>fibsUnfold</code>, <code>mapUnfold</code>, <code>takeUnfold</code>, <code>takeWhileUnfold</code>, <code>zipWith</code>.
@@s017 It is not Scala's LazyList
@@s018 At the top of the file: <i>"we are using our own lazy lists, not the standard library"</i>. <code>None</code>, <code>Some</code>, <code>Option</code>, <code>List</code>, <code>Nil</code> come from the standard library; the <code>LazyList</code>, however, is the course's own. The standard library has its own <code>scala.collection.immutable.LazyList</code> (with <code>#::</code> instead of <code>cons</code>, and also <code>from</code> and <code>unfold</code>), but it is not used in the exercises.
